{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Selective Availability deliberately degraded public GPS signals for national security.","label":"F0","sourceEvidence":"Selective Availability deliberately degraded public GPS signals for national security.","synthesisStatement":"The synthesis states the same purpose and effect.","narrativeStatement":"The narrative’s context states the same purpose and effect.","origin":"upstream","explanation":"Faithfully states what the feature did and its stated purpose.","materiality":"material"},{"excerpt":"In May 2000, at President Bill Clinton’s direction, the US government stopped using the feature, with the aim of making civil and commercial GPS more useful worldwide.","label":"F0","sourceEvidence":"In May 2000 the US government stopped using it at President Bill Clinton’s direction to make civil and commercial GPS more useful worldwide.","synthesisStatement":"Supports the date, direction, action, and stated aim.","narrativeStatement":"The narrative’s development states the same action, direction, and aim.","origin":"upstream","explanation":"The wording preserves the qualification that increased usefulness was the aim, not a quantified or guaranteed outcome.","materiality":"material"},{"excerpt":"A separate step followed in September 2007, when the government announced that future GPS III satellites would be procured without Selective Availability.","label":"F0","sourceEvidence":"In September 2007 the government announced it would procure the future GPS III generation without the feature.","synthesisStatement":"Supports the date, announcement, and future GPS III procurement scope.","narrativeStatement":"The narrative’s development states that future GPS III satellites would be procured without the feature.","origin":"upstream","explanation":"Correctly distinguishes the later procurement announcement from the earlier operational change.","materiality":"material"},{"excerpt":"The 2000 action ended the feature’s use under the existing policy; the 2007 pro